# REQUIRED PRODUCT FEATURES — MVP
## CSI ORIGIN 2026, PS-12

---

## CRITICAL USER JOURNEY

```
USER                    DCA participant (wallet holder)
   ↓
PROBLEM                 Idle capital earns nothing between intervals;
                         fixed-schedule execution ignores market conditions
   ↓
CORE PRODUCT ACTION      Deposit capital → held as ERC-4626 vault shares (earning yield)
                         → decision engine evaluates TWAP deviation + urgency
                         → coordinator withdraws only the needed tranche
   ↓
DECISION / OUTPUT        Execute full tranche / execute partial tranche / delay
                         (atomic: withdrawal + swap succeed together or not at all)
   ↓
USER ACTION              Deposit, set bounded constraints, observe/trigger execution
   ↓
BUSINESS OUTCOME         Capital earns yield while waiting AND avoids bad-condition
                         executions — provable as a $ delta vs. a naive scheduled bot
```

This journey is the only thing the MVP has to prove. Every feature below is graded against whether it's on this path.

---

## FEATURE CONTRACTS

---

### MUST HAVE

---

**1. Per-User Deposit & Vault Position (ERC-4626)**
- **Purpose:** Give each user an individual, yield-bearing capital position.
- **Target User:** DCA participant
- **Problem It Solves:** Idle capital currently earns nothing.
- **User Value:** Capital works for the user even before it's needed for a swap.
- **Priority:** MUST HAVE
- **User Story:** As a DCA participant, I want my deposited capital to earn yield automatically, so that waiting for my next execution doesn't cost me money.
- **Acceptance Criteria:** User deposits token → receives vault shares; share value increases over time without further action.
- **Success Metric:** Non-zero, growing yield balance visible per user between deposit and first execution.
- **Dependencies:** None (foundation layer).
- **Risks:** If real yield integration (e.g., Aave-wrapped vault) isn't stable in time, fall back to a simple, transparent accrual mechanism — must still be visibly real, not hardcoded.
- **Out of Scope:** Multiple yield strategies, strategy switching, real-world yield-source failure handling.
- **Why MVP fails without it:** This is half the core problem (idle capital). Without it there is no "yield-while-waiting" story at all — the product degrades to a plain scheduled swapper, which is a REJECTED artificial-differentiation position per the PS.

---

**2. Per-User Bounded DCA Constraints**
- **Purpose:** Let each user define the guardrails within which the system is allowed to act.
- **Target User:** DCA participant
- **Problem It Solves:** Without explicit bounds, "autonomous" execution is unconstrained market timing — explicitly out of scope per Context.
- **User Value:** User keeps control over frequency, size, and worst-case delay.
- **Priority:** MUST HAVE
- **User Story:** As a DCA participant, I want to set my frequency, target allocation, max delay, and tranche flex range, so the system never drifts from my intended plan.
- **Acceptance Criteria:** Parameters stored per user; system respects them in every decision.
- **Success Metric:** 100% of simulated decisions stay within configured bounds.
- **Dependencies:** Feature 1 (vault position to which constraints apply).
- **Risks:** Under-scoping the parameter set weakens the "bounded autonomy" claim central to the PS.
- **Out of Scope:** UI-driven parameter tuning wizards, presets/templates.
- **Why MVP fails without it:** "Bounded autonomy" is a named, non-negotiable requirement in the Context (§8). Without it, the product cannot claim to be safe or compliant with the PS — it becomes an unbounded trading bot, which is explicitly rejected scope.

---

**3. Deterministic Decision Engine (Execute Full / Partial / Delay)**
- **Purpose:** Convert market signals + urgency into one of the three allowed actions.
- **Target User:** DCA participant (indirect — this is the "brain," invisible but felt through outcomes)
- **Problem It Solves:** Rigid schedules execute blindly into bad conditions.
- **User Value:** Executions are timed/sized to avoid unnecessary cost.
- **Priority:** MUST HAVE
- **User Story:** As a DCA participant, I want the system to skip or shrink a purchase when conditions are bad, so I don't overpay relative to a blind schedule.
- **Acceptance Criteria:** Given TWAP deviation and urgency (days until max-delay), engine deterministically outputs one of: execute-full, execute-partial, delay. Same inputs → same output every time (auditability).
- **Success Metric:** Demo shows at least one delay/partial outcome and one full-execute outcome under two different simulated market states.
- **Dependencies:** Feature 2 (constraints define urgency ceiling and tranche flex range).
- **Risks:** Over-engineering the signal set (adding liquidity depth, full volatility modeling) burns hours for marginal demo value. **SIMPLIFY to TWAP deviation + urgency only** — this was already resolved in the prior strategy pass.
- **Out of Scope:** ML/statistical prediction (see AI/ML Handoff), MEV-aware signal weighting.
- **Why MVP fails without it:** This is the other half of the core problem (rigid timing). Without it, the "market-aware" claim in the PS title itself is unmet — the product is just a yield vault with a swap button.

---

**4. Atomic Withdraw + Swap Coordinator**
- **Purpose:** Guarantee the vault is never left in an inconsistent state.
- **Target User:** DCA participant (trust/safety requirement, mostly invisible unless it fails)
- **Problem It Solves:** A hook alone cannot reach into the vault (Context §5, FACT) — someone must own atomicity.
- **User Value:** No risk of capital being withdrawn but not swapped, or vice versa.
- **Priority:** MUST HAVE
- **User Story:** As a DCA participant, I want my withdrawal and swap to succeed or fail together, so my funds are never stuck in limbo.
- **Acceptance Criteria:** `withdraw()` and `swap()` occur in one transaction; a hook rejection reverts both.
- **Success Metric:** Forced-failure test shows vault position unchanged after a rejected swap attempt.
- **Dependencies:** Feature 1 (vault), Feature 6 (hook to enforce conditions).
- **Risks:** Getting revert semantics wrong is the single most likely silent bug in this build — must be explicitly tested, not assumed.
- **Out of Scope:** Multi-step partial-fill handling, cross-chain coordination.
- **Why MVP fails without it:** Atomicity is a named PS requirement (Context §5, §10). Without it, the architecture is technically non-compliant with the problem statement, regardless of how good the decision engine is — this is a disqualifying gap, not a nice-to-have.

---

**5. Uniswap v4 Hook — Execution Condition Enforcement**
- **Purpose:** Enforce execution-time bounds (tranche size, slippage/price-impact) at the moment of swap.
- **Target User:** DCA participant
- **Problem It Solves:** A decision made before the swap could still be executed into worse conditions than expected by the time the transaction lands.
- **User Value:** Hard guarantee that no swap executes outside acceptable bounds, regardless of what the off-chain/pre-swap logic decided.
- **Priority:** MUST HAVE
- **User Story:** As a DCA participant, I want the protocol to refuse to execute a swap that violates my slippage/impact limits, even at the last moment.
- **Acceptance Criteria:** Hook rejects (reverts) swap attempts exceeding configured bounds; allows compliant swaps through.
- **Success Metric:** Demo includes one deliberately-triggered rejection.
- **Dependencies:** Feature 4 (coordinator calls the pool through the hook).
- **Risks:** Hook logic complexity is easy to underestimate — keep enforcement rules to 2 conditions max (tranche cap, slippage/impact cap) for reliability.
- **Out of Scope:** Dynamic/adaptive threshold tuning, MEV-specific hook logic.
- **Why MVP fails without it:** Uniswap v4 hooks are a mandatory, named PS requirement. Without a working hook that actually enforces something, the "hook-based execution layer" claim is decorative — judges (especially the Technical Judge) will see through a hook that doesn't gate anything.

---

**6. Permissionless Trigger ("Poke") Function**
- **Purpose:** Cause the evaluate → act loop to actually run, since contracts don't run on their own.
- **Target User:** DCA participant (indirectly — this is infrastructure, not user-facing)
- **Problem It Solves:** The Context explicitly flags the trigger mechanism as unresolved and architecturally load-bearing; without one, "continuous monitoring" is meaningless.
- **User Value:** Enables the whole autonomous loop to function and be demoed live.
- **Priority:** MUST HAVE
- **User Story:** As anyone (keeper, script, or judge clicking a button), I want to call a check-and-execute function that evaluates a user's position and acts accordingly.
- **Acceptance Criteria:** Function callable by any address; correctly no-ops if the user isn't yet eligible.
- **Success Metric:** Live demo triggers a real state change on-chain via this function.
- **Dependencies:** Feature 3 (decision engine).
- **Risks:** None significant — this was the resolved forced decision from the prior strategy pass (poke function over live Chainlink/Gelato integration).
- **Out of Scope:** Real keeper-network integration (Chainlink Automation, Gelato) — narrate as a production path, don't build it.
- **Why MVP fails without it:** Without a trigger mechanism, nothing in the system ever executes. This is not optional infrastructure — it's the difference between a static contract and a working product.

---

**7. Multi-User Demo Support (≥2 distinct users/configs)**
- **Purpose:** Prove that "multi-user, per-position" (Context §2, a repeated FACT) is real, not decorative.
- **Target User:** DCA participant (proof point for the judging panel, not a solo-user feature)
- **Problem It Solves:** A single-user demo cannot distinguish "per-user logic" from "one global variable."
- **User Value:** Confidence that the system genuinely isolates and respects each user's own constraints and position.
- **Priority:** MUST HAVE
- **User Story:** As a judge, I want to see two users with different constraints produce different outcomes under the same market conditions, so I can trust the "multi-user" claim.
- **Acceptance Criteria:** Two users, different frequency/max-delay/tranche settings, same market snapshot → different logged actions.
- **Success Metric:** Visibly different decisions for user A vs. user B in the same demo run.
- **Dependencies:** Features 1–6 (must all work per-user).
- **Risks:** Low — mostly a matter of scripting the demo, not new engineering.
- **Out of Scope:** More than 2 users, dynamic user onboarding flow.
- **Why MVP fails without it:** The PS explicitly requires plural users (Context §2, non-negotiable FACT). A single-user demo directly contradicts a stated requirement and will be flagged by the Product and Technical judges.

